/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  totalReflux01_c6c7_yanagiRun628  --  a column at TOTAL REFLUX: no feed, no
  products, every mole of overhead vapour back as reflux.  This is how tray
  efficiency is measured on a test column, and it is the mode every later
  tray-efficiency comparison (C329: an efficiency computed from mass-transfer
  correlations and applied to equilibrium stages, not a rate-based model)
  stands on.

  WHAT IS SPECIFIED, AND WHY THESE NUMBERS (the degrees of freedom).  With no
  stream crossing the column boundary the component balances, summed over the
  stages, read 0 = 0: c of them are dependent, and what is left open is how
  much of each component circulates -- a test column fixes it by its charge
  and hold-up, which a steady stage model does not have.  So the case gives
  c numbers: the boil-up (one) and the liquid composition at one point (c - 1).
  Both come from a measured run:

    MEASURED INPUTS -- Yanagi & Sakata, Ind. Eng. Chem. Process Des. Dev. 21
    (1982) 712, Table III, run 628 (cyclohexane / n-heptane, 34 kPa):
      reboiler liquid           28.4 mol % cyclohexane
      vapour rate               1.69 kg/s at vapour density 1.03 kg/m3
                                -> u = 1.69/(1.03 x 1.131 m2) = 1.451 m/s on
                                   the 1.2 m tower, F = u sqrt(rho_V) = 1.47
      column                    1.2 m diameter, 10 trays (Table III numbers
                                the samples up to tray 10), top 34 kPa
    ESTIMATED PROPERTIES: everything else (Antoine, ideal liquid, Chung,
      Fuller, Brock-Bird, Rackett) -- see constant/thermoPhysPropDict.
    FITTED PARAMETERS: none.
    HELD-OUT POINTS: the measured profile of the same run, below.

  The trays are declared IDEAL here (no `murphreeEfficiency`, no `efficiency`
  block): this case is the total-reflux mode itself, and its own check is that
  ideal trays give an overall efficiency of 1 by the paper's own definition
  (the Fenske slope of ln(x/(1-x)) per tray over ln alpha) -- printed as the
  KPI `overallTrayEfficiency`, not 1 to the last digit only because alpha
  varies from the top tray to the bottom one.

  What the real trays did on that run (Table III): reflux 98.8 %, tray 10
  97.4 %, tray 8 93.7 %, tray 5 81.9 %, tray 4 73.2 %; overall tray efficiency
  70.8 %.  The ideal column here separates MORE than that, as it must.  The
  computed efficiency of the same trays is trayEfficiency01_yanagi14.

  NOT MODELLED: the pressure drop (0.393 kPa per tray measured) is not fed
  back, so every stage sits at 34 kPa and the reboiler is cooler than the
  measured 60.2 C; the reflux returns saturated (the run's was subcooled,
  38.3 C).

  THE HYDRAULICS here run on the default pressure-drop route (Sinnott) and
  print about 0.79 kPa per tray against the measured 0.393: Sinnott's residual
  head and full weir head do not see how aerated a froth at 34 kPa is.  The
  trayEfficiency cases declare `pressureDropModel BollesFair;` (Perry 9ed
  14-100..14-109), which prints 0.44 kPa on this run.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        column;
        type        distillationColumn;
        model       totalReflux;

        operation
        {
            nStages        11;          // 10 trays + the reboiler
            P              34 kPa;
            condenser      { type total; }

            totalReflux
            {
                fFactor        1.47;    // (m/s)(kg/m3)^0.5, reboiler vapour on the tower area
                specifiedAt    reboiler;
                composition
                {
                    cyclohexane    0.284;
                    nHeptane       0.716;
                }
            }

            hydraulics
            {
                trayType               sieve;
                diameter               1.2;      // m  (Table I)
                traySpacing            0.610;    // m  (Table I)
                weirHeight             0.0508;   // m  (Table I, efficiency runs)
                holeDiameter           0.0127;   // m  (Table I)
                holeAreaFraction       0.1387;   // 0.118 m2 holes / 0.851 m2 active (Table I)
                downcomerAreaFraction  0.1238;   // 0.14 m2 / 1.131 m2 (Table I)
                weirLengthFraction     0.7833;   // 0.940 m / 1.2 m (Table I)
                orificeCoefficient     0.693;    // Perry 9ed Eq 14-102a: 0.74(0.137) + exp(0.29(1.5/12.7) - 0.56)
            }
        }
    }
);
